# Sorting algorithms

Five classic comparison sorts, one card each, with the cost model they share and
the two properties that decide which one you actually reach for.

Read [[complexity]] first if the notation is unfamiliar; [[stability]] explains
the property that most often decides a real choice.

## The five

| Algorithm | Best | Average | Worst | Extra space | Stable |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [[bubble-sort]] | $O(n)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(1)$ | yes |
| [[insertion-sort]] | $O(n)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(1)$ | yes |
| [[merge-sort]] | $O(n\log n)$ | $O(n\log n)$ | $O(n\log n)$ | $O(n)$ | yes |
| [[quicksort]] | $O(n\log n)$ | $O(n\log n)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(\log n)$ | no |
| [[heapsort]] | $O(n\log n)$ | $O(n\log n)$ | $O(n\log n)$ | $O(1)$ | no |

!!! note "Why five, and why these"

    Bubble and insertion sort share a cost class but not a temperament, which
    makes them the clearest pair for showing that big-O is not the whole story.
    Merge, quick, and heap are the three ways to reach $O(n\log n)$, and each
    pays for it differently: memory, worst case, or constant factor.

## How they relate

```d2
direction: right

quadratic: "Quadratic: O(n²)" {
  bubble: Bubble sort
  insertion: Insertion sort
}

linearithmic: "Linearithmic: O(n log n)" {
  merge: Merge sort
  quick: Quicksort
  heap: Heapsort
}

quadratic.insertion -> linearithmic.merge: "divide instead of scan"
quadratic.insertion -> linearithmic.heap: "pick the minimum faster"
linearithmic.merge -> linearithmic.quick: "partition in place"
```

## Choosing one

!!! tip "The short version"

    Use your language's built-in sort. It is almost certainly a hybrid of
    [[merge-sort]] and [[insertion-sort]] that beats anything on this page.

When you cannot:

- **Small or nearly sorted input** — [[insertion-sort]]. Its $O(n)$ best case is
  real, and its constant factor is tiny.
- **You need [[stability]]** — [[merge-sort]].
- **Memory is tight and worst case matters** — [[heapsort]]. It is the only sort
  here that is both in-place and $O(n\log n)$ in the worst case.
- **Average speed matters most** — [[quicksort]], with a randomised pivot.

## The lower bound

No comparison sort beats $O(n\log n)$ in the worst case, and the argument is
short enough to state here:

